WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.376 --> 00:00:03.293
(speaking Spanish)

00:00:04.800 --> 00:00:06.950
- Hi, my name is Michael
and I'm Palestinian.

00:00:06.950 --> 00:00:09.290
- Hi, my name is Sheryl and I'm Mexican.

00:00:09.290 --> 00:00:11.530
- And today we're going
to be swapping meals.

00:00:11.530 --> 00:00:12.880
- What did you pick for me?

00:00:12.880 --> 00:00:14.910
Cause this is a lot of meat for one girl.

00:00:14.910 --> 00:00:17.810
- Today you're going to be
eating something called Sfeeha,

00:00:17.810 --> 00:00:18.920
which is a meat pie.

00:00:18.920 --> 00:00:21.300
Usually sfeeha comes in smaller pies,

00:00:21.300 --> 00:00:24.320
but this place obviously
loves large portions.

00:00:24.320 --> 00:00:26.700
And then you're going
to be eating shawarma.

00:00:26.700 --> 00:00:29.300
- The dish I chose for
you, it is called tclayuda.

00:00:29.300 --> 00:00:32.750
It has cabbage, beans,
quesillo which is what

00:00:32.750 --> 00:00:35.520
we're known for like a
huge long string cheese.

00:00:35.520 --> 00:00:37.940
It's the best part,
honestly, of the tclayudas.

00:00:37.940 --> 00:00:41.100
And I also ordered you a Taco Al Pastor.

00:00:41.100 --> 00:00:42.890
So I'm really excited for you to try it.

00:00:42.890 --> 00:00:45.020
- Alright. Let's get into it.

00:00:45.020 --> 00:00:46.908
- This is a shwarma, how do you say it?

00:00:46.908 --> 00:00:48.260
- Shawarma.

00:00:48.260 --> 00:00:49.221
- Shawarma.

00:00:49.221 --> 00:00:51.770
- Yes, you go it. You sound authentic.

00:00:51.770 --> 00:00:54.250
- This is so big. How do
I even start eating it?

00:00:54.250 --> 00:00:55.420
- I mean, honestly, you have options.

00:00:55.420 --> 00:00:57.020
You can like fold it up and bite it.

00:00:57.020 --> 00:00:58.060
You could use a fork,

00:00:58.060 --> 00:00:59.997
like whatever you're
most comfortable with.

00:00:59.997 --> 00:01:00.830
- Like this?

00:01:00.830 --> 00:01:01.688
- I like to fold it up and bite it.

00:01:01.688 --> 00:01:03.087
- Are they typically, like, this big?

00:01:03.087 --> 00:01:03.920
(Michael laughs)

00:01:03.920 --> 00:01:05.145
- It's not usually that much meat.

00:01:05.145 --> 00:01:07.020
That seems like a pretty large portion.

00:01:07.020 --> 00:01:09.437
(soft music)

00:01:10.860 --> 00:01:12.740
- It's really, like, flavorful.

00:01:12.740 --> 00:01:16.010
So I know, like, they marinated
this, but it's really good.

00:01:16.010 --> 00:01:17.020
It's like sweet.

00:01:17.020 --> 00:01:19.290
- When my family and I go
back to the Middle East,

00:01:19.290 --> 00:01:21.830
my mom's always like, "Got
to get some shawarma spices"

00:01:21.830 --> 00:01:23.320
and you know, we get them vacuum sealed

00:01:23.320 --> 00:01:26.010
and we take them home and
she'll throw it in the freezer

00:01:26.010 --> 00:01:28.060
and she'll use it to make
her own shawarma at home.

00:01:28.060 --> 00:01:29.820
- I went into the store and I'm like,

00:01:29.820 --> 00:01:32.370
these spices look so good, like how?

00:01:32.370 --> 00:01:34.660
How can I not have
these spices in my life?

00:01:34.660 --> 00:01:37.620
- And also shawarma typically is cooked on

00:01:37.620 --> 00:01:39.520
like a vertical rotisserie.

00:01:39.520 --> 00:01:42.250
So there's a large history
of Arab immigration

00:01:42.250 --> 00:01:44.130
to Latin America specifically.

00:01:44.130 --> 00:01:46.995
So you'll see a lot of Arab influence

00:01:46.995 --> 00:01:48.530
in a lot of Latino dishes.

00:01:48.530 --> 00:01:51.740
- Mexicans got inspired by
that and actually took, like,

00:01:51.740 --> 00:01:54.010
cooking their meat on the stick.

00:01:54.010 --> 00:01:56.290
And that's how we have
al pastor tacos now.

00:01:56.290 --> 00:02:00.100
- Me and my Latinx friends
have always kind of bonded over

00:02:00.100 --> 00:02:02.200
the similarities in our
food and our culture

00:02:02.200 --> 00:02:03.980
and just like our love for music

00:02:03.980 --> 00:02:05.340
and like our love for parties.

00:02:05.340 --> 00:02:06.250
We love a good party.

00:02:06.250 --> 00:02:07.420
- Yes!

00:02:07.420 --> 00:02:09.680
- And I'm eating this taco al pastor

00:02:11.580 --> 00:02:12.780
and I'm like, this is so good.

00:02:12.780 --> 00:02:14.380
Especially with the salsa.

00:02:14.380 --> 00:02:15.380
Oh my God, it's so good.

00:02:15.380 --> 00:02:18.810
The tacos al pastor actually
do resemble shawarma to me.

00:02:18.810 --> 00:02:21.610
They kind of have a similar
taste and a similar texture.

00:02:21.610 --> 00:02:22.660
All right, let me finish this taco

00:02:22.660 --> 00:02:24.800
and then I want to try
this tclayuda so bad.

00:02:24.800 --> 00:02:27.380
- I would say I'ma
finish this, but I'm not.

00:02:27.380 --> 00:02:28.620
It's too much.

00:02:28.620 --> 00:02:29.890
- I just love tacos.

00:02:29.890 --> 00:02:34.281
- That was a lot of meat, I
need to drink my rose drink.

00:02:34.281 --> 00:02:35.160
(top pops)

00:02:35.160 --> 00:02:36.500
I've never had rose drink.

00:02:36.500 --> 00:02:38.560
I feel like just, the name is pretty.

00:02:38.560 --> 00:02:42.960
I just envision myself like in
my bathtub with rose petals.

00:02:42.960 --> 00:02:45.090
I don't think this is the
same, like me drinking

00:02:45.090 --> 00:02:46.345
my bath water.

00:02:46.345 --> 00:02:47.490
(sips drink)

00:02:47.490 --> 00:02:49.100
Oh my gosh, it's so sweet.

00:02:49.100 --> 00:02:51.260
I'm just going to finish
it. Okay I'm done.

00:02:51.260 --> 00:02:54.340
- Our countries and our
regions that we're from

00:02:54.340 --> 00:02:56.320
people really know how to like,

00:02:56.320 --> 00:03:00.210
use the land and like use
the resources available.

00:03:00.210 --> 00:03:01.650
As like a lot of other cultures do

00:03:01.650 --> 00:03:04.100
but it's not in my experience
as common of a thing

00:03:04.100 --> 00:03:07.160
in like in America to like
drink rosewater, for example.

00:03:07.160 --> 00:03:08.640
People know rosewater exists

00:03:08.640 --> 00:03:10.120
and like they will use the toner.

00:03:10.120 --> 00:03:10.953
I use the toner.

00:03:10.953 --> 00:03:13.560
I need to drink my horchata.

00:03:13.560 --> 00:03:14.945
(Sheryl claps)

00:03:14.945 --> 00:03:15.940
- Yes.

00:03:15.940 --> 00:03:18.250
So it has cinnamon, it has milk.

00:03:18.250 --> 00:03:20.278
It's sweet. It's refreshing.

00:03:20.278 --> 00:03:21.607
- Smells like Cinnamon Toast Crunch.

00:03:21.607 --> 00:03:22.890
- So it's like a good, it's agua fresca.

00:03:22.890 --> 00:03:25.580
So the name means like fresh water.

00:03:25.580 --> 00:03:27.800
So it's supposed to like refresh you.

00:03:27.800 --> 00:03:30.277
And it's so good for the summer hot time.

00:03:30.277 --> 00:03:33.194
(speaking Spanish)

00:03:35.520 --> 00:03:38.080
- I love things that
taste kind of like cereal.

00:03:38.080 --> 00:03:40.687
So me making that connection like really,

00:03:40.687 --> 00:03:42.859
really just elevates it for me.

00:03:42.859 --> 00:03:44.480
(sipping drink)

00:03:44.480 --> 00:03:45.500
- Mm-mm.

00:03:45.500 --> 00:03:47.744
- I need to take a break cause
I'm about to finish this.

00:03:47.744 --> 00:03:48.577
(Sheryl laughs)

00:03:48.577 --> 00:03:50.230
- We need to get to the tclayuda

00:03:50.230 --> 00:03:54.380
because that is gonna be a
beast to even get through.

00:03:54.380 --> 00:03:55.380
It's like a huge pizza.

00:03:55.380 --> 00:03:57.800
And I don't know if they
cut it into like slices.

00:03:57.800 --> 00:03:58.633
- You know what?

00:03:58.633 --> 00:04:01.770
I think they technically did,
but I'm going to grab a knife.

00:04:01.770 --> 00:04:03.038
Zing!

00:04:03.038 --> 00:04:03.871
(knife cutting)

00:04:03.871 --> 00:04:04.704
I love tortillas.

00:04:04.704 --> 00:04:07.191
I think a crunchy tortilla
is, like, a godsend.

00:04:07.191 --> 00:04:08.690
(heavenly music)

00:04:08.690 --> 00:04:10.447
- I love the beef and the chorizo flavor.

00:04:10.447 --> 00:04:11.988
The quesillo is good.

00:04:11.988 --> 00:04:15.563
Avocado, I have like a strange
relationship with avocado.

00:04:16.580 --> 00:04:17.880
It's complicated.

00:04:17.880 --> 00:04:20.160
Y'all got to get you a
tclayuda, because this is good.

00:04:20.160 --> 00:04:21.553
Especially with the salsa.

00:04:21.553 --> 00:04:24.320
(romantic music)

00:04:24.320 --> 00:04:26.400
- I'm going to try this sfeeha,

00:04:26.400 --> 00:04:28.730
which is also known as a meat pie.

00:04:28.730 --> 00:04:32.497
And I already love it
because I love thin crust

00:04:32.497 --> 00:04:34.270
(food crunches)

00:04:34.270 --> 00:04:36.610
So much flavor! I love that.

00:04:36.610 --> 00:04:38.420
It's not super greasy either.

00:04:38.420 --> 00:04:42.522
And then we have onion
and tomato and meat.

00:04:42.522 --> 00:04:43.510
I love meat.

00:04:43.510 --> 00:04:47.360
So anything with like a
bread and meat, perfect.

00:04:47.360 --> 00:04:48.770
I always eat the same thing

00:04:48.770 --> 00:04:50.320
because I'm afraid of new things.

00:04:50.320 --> 00:04:52.660
But I think when you take a risk

00:04:52.660 --> 00:04:54.630
and like learn about different cultures.

00:04:54.630 --> 00:04:58.007
Now I have so many other
food choices to choose from.

00:04:58.007 --> 00:05:01.525
- My heart. I absolutely
would eat a tclayuda again.

00:05:01.525 --> 00:05:03.820
And now I know a spot
where to get tclayuda.

00:05:03.820 --> 00:05:06.060
And I'll honestly probably tell
my friends to get some, too.

00:05:06.060 --> 00:05:07.130
- Y'all can share.

00:05:07.130 --> 00:05:09.867
- Yes. I'm going to
need to share with them.

00:05:10.740 --> 00:05:11.935
This is huge!

00:05:11.935 --> 00:05:12.985
(Michael chuckles)

00:05:12.985 --> 00:05:15.568
(upbeat music)

